= Rubber rice pact Expires

The Ceylon-China trade agreement has officially expired. Signed all the way back in 1952 the agreement was off significant importance. At the time Sri Lanka was experiencing a dual crisis as the global price of rice had dramatically risen leading to a shortage domestically.

At the same time the advent of synthetic rubber and the end of the Korean war led to the price of rubber to crater, depriving the economy of a major export and a large abundance. The newly formed Peoples Republic of China also faced issues.

Having gone through decades of war and civil conflict, the CCP under Mao Zedong was embarking on an ambitious development and modernisation plan. For this they required among other things, rubber. However the international climate was not supportive of the new regime.

China would be essentially cut off from international trade by the United States and its allies, combined with a United Nations resolution barring the sale of rubber to China.

With this climate, the UNP led government under Dudley Senanayake initially requested the United States for a loan and favourable rubber prices. This was rejected.

Minister of Commerce RG Senanayake then proposed to trade with China to solve both crises. Dudley agreed and the agreement was signed, Sri Lanka was now selling rubber to China at above market price and buying rice at below market price.

The agreement however, enraged the Americans who would envoked the Battle Act cutting Sri Lanka off from US aid, which would hamper the economy.

Over time the agreement kept being extended, a total of 5 times. Now however the agreement is not as important as it was before, China can now import rubber from other nations and Sri Lanka has achieved near self sufficiency in rice production.

It is no surprise then that the agreement was allowed to expire.
